1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,181
Late last year, Microsoft's

2
00:00:03,181 --> 00:00:05,363
CEO said something strange.

3
00:00:05,613 --> 00:00:08,211
His company may have a bunch of AI chips

4
00:00:08,211 --> 00:00:11,334
sitting in inventory — that it can't plug in.

5
00:00:11,584 --> 00:00:13,303
The chips weren't the problem.

6
00:00:13,303 --> 00:00:15,622
The problem, in his words, was power.

7
00:00:15,872 --> 00:00:17,257
So today's question:

8
00:00:17,257 --> 00:00:20,364
will electricity become the ceiling on AI?

9
00:00:20,664 --> 00:00:23,822
My answer is yes. But not in the way you think.

10
00:00:24,622 --> 00:00:26,199
Start with the size of it.

11
00:00:26,449 --> 00:00:29,633
In 2024, data centers used about

12
00:00:29,633 --> 00:00:33,216
415 terawatt-hours of electricity.

13
00:00:33,466 --> 00:00:35,618
That's around one and a half percent

14
00:00:35,618 --> 00:00:38,090
of all the electricity the world uses.

15
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:41,667
One and a half percent doesn't sound like a crisis.

16
00:00:41,917 --> 00:00:46,668
But the International Energy Agency expects it to more

17
00:00:46,668 --> 00:00:51,268
than double by 2030, to around 945 terawatt-hours.

18
00:00:51,518 --> 00:00:54,695
That's slightly more than all of Japan uses today.

19
00:00:54,945 --> 00:00:59,073
And it's concentrated. The United States alone accounts

20
00:00:59,073 --> 00:01:02,875
for about 45 percent of data center electricity.

21
00:01:03,125 --> 00:01:06,510
Through 2030, data centers are expected to drive

22
00:01:06,510 --> 00:01:10,519
nearly half of America's growth in electricity demand.

23
00:01:11,319 --> 00:01:13,057
Here's what most people miss.

24
00:01:13,657 --> 00:01:16,183
The world isn't running out of electricity.

25
00:01:16,183 --> 00:01:18,674
It's running out of time to connect it.

26
00:01:18,924 --> 00:01:21,160
A data center is just a building.

27
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:24,208
Feeding it takes a whole chain: power plants,

28
00:01:24,208 --> 00:01:26,644
transmission lines, transformers.

29
00:01:26,894 --> 00:01:29,849
And every link in that chain has a waiting line.

30
00:01:30,099 --> 00:01:32,331
New transmission lines in advanced

31
00:01:32,331 --> 00:01:35,419
economies take four to eight years to build.

32
00:01:35,669 --> 00:01:38,376
Large power transformers now have lead

33
00:01:38,376 --> 00:01:41,368
times of up to four years, and prices have

34
00:01:41,368 --> 00:01:44,417
jumped roughly 80 percent in five years.

35
00:01:44,667 --> 00:01:48,667
Gas turbines? GE Vernova, one of the biggest makers,

36
00:01:48,667 --> 00:01:53,020
said late last year that it was sold out through 2028.

37
00:01:53,270 --> 00:01:55,715
The IEA estimates that, unless these

38
00:01:55,715 --> 00:01:58,634
bottlenecks are fixed, around 20 percent of

39
00:01:58,634 --> 00:02:01,958
planned data center projects could be delayed.

40
00:02:02,208 --> 00:02:06,433
Morgan Stanley put a number on the gap: a shortfall of

41
00:02:06,433 --> 00:02:10,702
about 32 gigawatts for US data centers through 2028.

42
00:02:10,952 --> 00:02:13,268
That's roughly a third of what they need.

43
00:02:14,068 --> 00:02:16,861
When something is scarce, its price tells you.

44
00:02:17,111 --> 00:02:20,296
PJM runs the power grid across all or

45
00:02:20,296 --> 00:02:24,112
parts of 13 US states and Washington, D.C.

46
00:02:24,362 --> 00:02:26,301
Every year, it holds an auction

47
00:02:26,301 --> 00:02:28,002
for future power supply.

48
00:02:28,252 --> 00:02:31,328
For 2024 to 2025, the price was

49
00:02:31,328 --> 00:02:34,901
about 29 dollars per megawatt-day.

50
00:02:35,151 --> 00:02:38,945
For 2027 to 2028, it hit 333 dollars,

51
00:02:38,945 --> 00:02:42,221
the maximum the rules allowed.

52
00:02:42,471 --> 00:02:45,004
Analysts estimate that without the cap,

53
00:02:45,004 --> 00:02:47,283
it would have been close to 530.

54
00:02:47,533 --> 00:02:50,048
And even at that price, the auction still

55
00:02:50,048 --> 00:02:52,518
fell short of its reliability target.

56
00:02:52,768 --> 00:02:58,659
The reason? PJM's demand forecast jumped by 5,250 megawatts,

57
00:02:58,659 --> 00:03:02,787
almost entirely because of data centers.

58
00:03:03,037 --> 00:03:05,686
And that cost doesn't stay with tech companies.

59
00:03:05,936 --> 00:03:08,781
In Washington, D.C., one utility's

60
00:03:08,781 --> 00:03:12,296
residential customers saw bills rise about

61
00:03:12,296 --> 00:03:15,727
21 dollars a month starting in June 2025,

62
00:03:15,727 --> 00:03:19,526
around 10 of it from these capacity prices.

63
00:03:20,326 --> 00:03:22,289
So the tech giants stopped waiting.

64
00:03:22,539 --> 00:03:26,435
Microsoft signed a deal to bring back a reactor

65
00:03:26,435 --> 00:03:30,248
at Three Mile Island, which shut down in 2019.

66
00:03:30,248 --> 00:03:33,598
It's now expected back online in 2027.

67
00:03:33,848 --> 00:03:36,113
Others are skipping the grid entirely.

68
00:03:36,363 --> 00:03:40,062
One analysis counted 59 US data center projects

69
00:03:40,062 --> 00:03:44,591
planning about 90 gigawatts of their own on-site power.

70
00:03:44,841 --> 00:03:47,206
That's more than a quarter of all planned

71
00:03:47,206 --> 00:03:49,482
data center capacity in the country.

72
00:03:49,732 --> 00:03:52,458
And 92 percent of those projects were

73
00:03:52,458 --> 00:03:55,163
announced since the start of 2025.

74
00:03:55,413 --> 00:04:00,531
It got strange. Crusoe, a company that builds AI data centers,

75
00:04:00,531 --> 00:04:04,245
ordered 29 gas turbines from Boom Supersonic,

76
00:04:04,245 --> 00:04:08,655
a startup best known for designing supersonic jets.

77
00:04:08,905 --> 00:04:11,379
Then, just last month, Crusoe walked

78
00:04:11,379 --> 00:04:13,647
away from that deal and turned to

79
00:04:13,647 --> 00:04:16,459
established suppliers like GE Vernova.

80
00:04:16,709 --> 00:04:18,670
When time is the bottleneck,

81
00:04:18,670 --> 00:04:22,022
an untested machine is a risk few can afford.

82
00:04:22,822 --> 00:04:24,115
Now flip the map.

83
00:04:24,365 --> 00:04:26,758
China generates more than twice as

84
00:04:26,758 --> 00:04:29,633
much electricity as the United States.

85
00:04:29,883 --> 00:04:33,284
In 2025 alone, it added more than 430

86
00:04:33,284 --> 00:04:36,885
gigawatts of wind and solar capacity.

87
00:04:37,135 --> 00:04:40,160
BloombergNEF expects China to add more

88
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:43,504
than six times as much generation capacity

89
00:04:43,504 --> 00:04:46,490
as the US over the next five years.

90
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:49,898
One business school researcher put it simply:

91
00:04:49,898 --> 00:04:52,916
the US has the chips and is short on power.

92
00:04:52,916 --> 00:04:56,064
China has the power and is short on chips.

93
00:04:56,314 --> 00:04:58,379
So electricity isn't one ceiling.

94
00:04:58,379 --> 00:05:02,020
It's a different ceiling, depending on where you stand.

95
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:04,271
So here's my call.

96
00:05:04,521 --> 00:05:06,298
Electricity won't stop AI.

97
00:05:06,298 --> 00:05:09,778
Too much money is chasing this problem for that.

98
00:05:10,028 --> 00:05:12,615
But for the next few years, power will

99
00:05:12,615 --> 00:05:15,271
decide three things: how fast AI grows,

100
00:05:15,271 --> 00:05:17,786
where it gets built, and who wins.

101
00:05:18,036 --> 00:05:21,190
The winners won't just be the ones with the best models.

102
00:05:21,190 --> 00:05:23,981
They'll be the ones who locked in power early.

103
00:05:24,231 --> 00:05:26,738
I'd go further: by the end of this decade,

104
00:05:26,738 --> 00:05:29,186
a guaranteed grid connection may be worth

105
00:05:29,186 --> 00:05:31,595
more than the chips you plug into it.

106
00:05:32,395 --> 00:05:33,987
What does this mean for you?

107
00:05:34,237 --> 00:05:36,999
If you live where data centers are booming,

108
00:05:36,999 --> 00:05:40,925
part of AI's bill may already be on your electricity bill.

109
00:05:41,175 --> 00:05:44,694
And next time someone says AI is limited by chips,

110
00:05:44,694 --> 00:05:46,736
remember Microsoft's problem.

111
00:05:46,736 --> 00:05:49,681
The chips were there. The power wasn't.

112
00:05:49,931 --> 00:05:52,650
So which do you think runs out first: chips,

113
00:05:52,650 --> 00:05:55,321
or electricity? Tell me in the comments.
